LEAD = {
    "headline": "TWITCH'S CHIEF EXECUTIVE PUT A NUMBER ON WHAT A LIVE SPONSOR READ ACTUALLY REACHES.  FIFTEEN PERCENT OF THE CREATOR'S COMMUNITY",
    "deck": "Dan Clancy said it out loud yesterday, on the record, while announcing the product he says will fix it.  Nobody on the brand side has been pricing live sponsorship against that number, because until yesterday nobody at the platform had published it.  This is the rare case of a seller handing you the argument for paying him less.",
    "stamps": [
        ("GAMESBEAT · 29 SEP", "https://gamesbeat.com/twitch-is-building-a-new-recaps-feed-for-creators-highlight-reels/"),
        ("TUBEFILTER · 29 SEP", "https://www.tubefilter.com/2026/09/29/twitch-clips-recaps-feed-streamer-highlights/"),
    ],
    "body": [
        "Alexander Lee interviewed Clancy at Tokyo Game Show and published it yesterday.  The news peg is a product: Twitch is building a Recaps Feed, where a creator picks five to eight clips at the end of a stream and Twitch stitches them into one highlight reel.  That is mildly interesting.  The two sentences buried underneath it are not.",
        "Here is the first.  <mark>\"From a viewer perspective, at any given moment of a stream, probably only 10 percent of your community is there, right?\"</mark>  And here is the one that should go straight into your next negotiation.  <mark>\"That will help our creators, because now, let's say someone does a sponsorship on Twitch — it might only be 15 percent of their community that was there during the sponsorship.  Create a clip from different pieces, redistribute it on Twitch, and you get greater reach, and that will help them make more money.\"</mark>",
        "Read that again as a buyer rather than a reader.  Every livestream sponsorship you have ever priced was priced off something that looks like audience size — follower count, average concurrent viewers, a rate the talent's agent gave you.  The man who runs the platform has just said that the segment you paid for was seen by roughly fifteen percent of the community you thought you were buying.  Not fifteen percent of the internet.  Fifteen percent of the specific audience that is the entire reason you chose that creator.",
        "The mechanism is obvious once it is stated, which is why it has gone unstated for a decade.  A livestream is a moment, not a library.  A four-hour broadcast has one audience at the start and a different one at the end, and the sponsor read happens at a single point inside it.  Everything else you buy in video — a YouTube integration, a television spot, a pre-roll — accumulates.  A live read does not.  It happens once, to whoever is in the room, and then it is gone unless somebody cuts it out.",
        "Which is exactly what Twitch is now volunteering to do, and you should notice the shape of the offer.  Clancy says the company wants to help creators use clips to deliver sponsored content, and that the reels <mark>\"will live on in perpetuity.\"</mark>  So the fix for the fifteen percent is a redistribution product, owned by the platform, shipping in what Clancy called \"the coming weeks.\"  Nobody has said whether inclusion of a sponsored moment in a Recap is free, whether the sponsor gets a say, or whose disclosure obligation the clip carries.",
        "One honest caveat on the number itself.  Fifteen percent is Clancy's figure, given conversationally, with no methodology, no definition of community, and no date range.  It is almost certainly a directional estimate rather than a measurement, and it will vary enormously between a creator who streams twelve hours a day and one who streams a two-hour event.  Do not put it in a deck as a benchmark.  Put it in a question instead, which is the point of it.",
        "Because there is a second thing worth doing with this, and it is not about Twitch.  The same arithmetic applies to every live thing you have ever sponsored — a co-stream, a launch broadcast, a shopping stream, a conference keynote.  You have been buying a room and reporting a reach.  Ask the question everywhere.",
    ],
    "numbers": [
        ("15%", "of a creator's community that was present during the sponsored segment, per Twitch's chief executive"),
        ("10%", "of a community watching at any single moment of a stream, same interview"),
        ("5-8", "clips a creator picks after a stream, which Twitch assembles into one reel it says will live on in perpetuity"),
    ],
    "so_what": "Live sponsorship has been sold on the size of the community and delivered against the size of the moment, and the gap between those two things was never anybody's line item.  Now the platform has named it.  The practical consequence is not that Twitch is worse value than you thought — it may be better, if you get the redistribution for free — it is that you have been measuring the wrong object, and so has the person who sold it to you.",
    "do_this": "Go back to your last three live sponsorships and ask the seller for the concurrent viewer count during the sponsored segment specifically, not the broadcast average, then write Recap and clip inclusion into your next live contract as a deliverable you own rather than an extra you buy later.",
}

