                    "That is the trap in one example.  Hours watched goes up when an event runs longer, adds platforms, or is simply streamed by more people — none of which means more humans watched.  Per channel, the 2024 game did around 3,100 hours and this one did around 470.  The windows and platform sets behind those two figures are not identical, so treat the ratio as a direction rather than a measurement, but the direction is not in doubt.",
                    "The brand-relevant bit is the marketing, and it is a physical object.  <mark>Screen Burn sent roughly 100 limited, press-exclusive functioning \"CRTV\" replica props to selected creators ahead of launch</mark>, and Konami timed a 2000 AD one-shot comic to the release window.  Sponsored streams then carried 3.54 percent of total watch time, which Streams Charts calls above average — against the 2.33 percent it called typical for EA Sports FC 27's launch.  A hundred props, and a sponsored share half again as high as a much larger game managed.",
